    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Interpret simple phrases from a text. Communication: Describe everyday objects using visual support.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret simple phrases from a text by pointing to related visuals (e.g., points to "head" when hearing "Touch your head."). Can describe everyday objects using visual support (e.g., when asked, "What do you wear on rainy days?" the student responds, "I wear my red raincoat." When shown a picture and asked, "What's this?" the student responds, "It's my red raincoat."). Can use simple gestures to clarify descriptions for peers (e.g., points to "hand" and says "hand").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game. The teacher says "Simon Says, touch your head!" or "Simon Says, put on your hat!" (mime putting on a hat) using flashcards or realia (hat, shoes). Students follow the instructions by touching their body parts or miming. This activates prior knowledge of body parts and clothing items using imperatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a flashcard of a child wearing a hat. The teacher points to the child's head and says, "This is her head. She wears a hat on her head." The teacher models the pronunciation of /h/ in "hat" and /t/ in "toes" while pointing to the corresponding body parts or items on other flashcards. The teacher demonstrates how to interpret a simple description by pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a hat flashcard: "Is this a shoe or a hat?" (Expected: "A hat!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to their head: "What is this?" (Expected: "Head!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says, "Put on your hat!" and points to their head. "Did I put it on my foot or my head?" (Expected: "Head!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About My Body".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated story about puppets getting dressed, titled "My Puppet's Clothes." The story is simple, with one sentence per page, paired with a clear visual. For example:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Page 1: (Picture of a puppet with a hat) "This is my puppet. It wears a hat on its head."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Page 2: (Picture of the puppet with shoes) "It wears shoes on its feet."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Page 3: (Picture of the puppet with a jacket) "It wears a jacket on its arm."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediation: Text: Interpret simple phrases from a text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> After reading, the teacher asks, "What does the puppet wear on its head?" (Students point to the hat picture or say "Hat!"). "What does it wear on its feet?" (Students point to shoes or say "Shoes!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher shows two large flashcards: one with a hat and one with shoes. The teacher reads a sentence from the story, "It wears a hat on its head." Students point to the corresponding flashcard (the hat). Repeat with other clothing items.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpret simple phrases, describe objects using visuals. Grammar: Imperative + noun, Present simple ("It wears..."). Vocabulary: head, shoes, hat, jacket, arm, feet, wears, on.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice: Picture-Sentence Match:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with pictures of different puppets, each wearing one item of clothing (e.g., a puppet with a hat, a puppet with shoes). Below the pictures are simple sentences (e.g., "This puppet wears a hat on its head." "This puppet wears shoes on its feet."). Students draw a line to match each sentence to the correct puppet picture, interpreting the text to visual information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled: Listen and Draw/Point:</b> The teacher describes a puppet or a flashcard image. "This puppet wears a red hat on its head and blue shoes on its feet." Students listen and either draw the items on a blank puppet outline on their paper, or point to the correct items on a pre-drawn puppet if available. The teacher can use WH-questions like "Where is the hat?" to prompt.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction: Describe and Interpret:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a flashcard of a different puppet wearing various clothes. Student A describes their puppet to Student B using simple sentences (e.g., "My puppet wears a yellow hat on its head." "It wears green socks on its feet."). Student B listens and points to the described items on their own flashcard or attempts to draw it based on the description, actively interpreting the peer's language. Students switch roles. The teacher encourages using gestures to clarify ("This is my hand," pointing to their hand) if a peer doesn't understand.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About My Body".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Puppet Show Presentation (21st Century Skills Project: Body Part Puppet Show).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students present their self-made puppets and the related storybook pages to their peers in a small "puppet show." Each student stands with their puppet and points to the body parts and clothing items while describing them. The focus is on them mediating their creative work (puppet and storybook) to their audience.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students, in groups of 3-4, take turns presenting their puppets and a chosen page from their storybook (e.g., the page describing what their puppet wears).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Each student introduces their puppet and uses simple sentences to describe its body parts and clothing, pointing to the specific items as they speak. Examples: "This is my puppet, Happy Ted. He wears a red hat on his head. He wears big shoes on his feet." They can also say, "This is his arm," while pointing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Peers in the audience listen and, as a mediation task, are encouraged to point to the described body parts/clothing on their own bodies or on a puppet drawing if they have one. The teacher facilitates by asking questions like, "What does Happy Ted wear on his head?" to encourage audience interpretation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The teacher circulates, offering support and prompting students to use the target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "Where is his jacket?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Formative assessment strategy.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the puppet show presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their puppet and explain what it wears, pointing to body parts and clothes. They also demonstrate interpreting a peer's description by pointing to specific items.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /h/ in "hat" and /t/ in "toes."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., hat, shoes, head, feet, wears).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of description (e.g., "My puppet wears a hat on its head.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to use simple gestures to clarify descriptions for peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to interpret a peer's description by pointing to visuals.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple worksheet with two questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. I can describe my puppet's clothes. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. I can point to my friend's puppet's hat when they say "hat." (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What did you like about making your puppet and describing its clothes?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Was it easy or difficult to understand your friends when they described their puppets? Why?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? The puppet show effectively motivated students to describe and interpret. The use of gestures was a strong support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next time I teach this content? More explicit practice with "Where is/are...?" questions for peer-to-peer interpretation. Ensure all students have ample opportunity to both describe and interpret. Perhaps provide sentence starters for audience members (e.g., "Where is the ____?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is Lesson 5, the final lesson in the sequence for "Hats and Shoes!" and "All About My Body." The next lesson will transition to a new theme and set of vocabulary, building on students' ability to understand and produce simple descriptive language in context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>